{"name":"Blue Thunder","hex":"#677f86","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"thunder","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The loud rumbling, cracking, or crashing sound caused by expansion of rapidly heated air around a lightning bolt.","A deep, rumbling noise resembling thunder."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce thunder; to sound, rattle, or roar, as a discharge of atmospheric electricity.","To make a noise like thunder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/thunder"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Thunder","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Thunder is a 1983 American action thriller film directed by John Badham and starring Roy Scheider, Malcolm McDowell, Daniel Stern, Candy Clark and Warren Oates. It depicts a Los Angeles police officer (Scheider) who discovers a conspiracy to dominate the city with an advanced military helicopter."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f3/Blue_Thunder.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Thunder","wikidata":"Q1171141"},{"title":"Blue Thunder (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Thunder is an American action drama television series based on the movie of the same title that aired on ABC from January 6 until April 16, 1984, featuring the Blue Thunder helicopter."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c6/Blue_thunder_dvd.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Thunder_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q969171"},{"title":"Thunder","blocks":[{"text":"Thunder is the sound caused by lightning. Depending upon the distance from and nature of the lightning, it can range from a long, low rumble to a sudden, loud crack. The sudden increase in temperature and hence pressure caused by the lightning produces rapid expansion of the air in the path of a lightning bolt. In turn, this expansion of air creates a sonic shock wave, often referred to as a \"thunderclap\" or \"peal of thunder\". The scientific study of thunder is known as brontology and the irrational fear (phobia) of thunder is called brontophobia."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunder","wikidata":"Q83301"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Blue_Thunder_1999_Left_View.jpg/500px-Blue_Thunder_1999_Left_View.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Blue_Thunder_1999_Left_View.jpg/1280px-Blue_Thunder_1999_Left_View.jpg?utm_source